{{Year article header|2026}}
The early part of the year saw escalating armed conflicts in the Americas and Middle East. In January, the United States launched [[2026 United States intervention in Venezuela|a military operation]] in Venezuela that captured sitting [[President of Venezuela|President]] [[Nicolás Maduro]], leading to [[United States oil blockade during Operation Southern Spear|a blockade]] of [[Venezuela|Venezuelan]] oil exports that contributed to [[2026 Cuban crisis|a crisis]] in [[Cuba]]. In [[Syria]], [[2026 northeastern Syria offensive|an offensive]] by the Syrian government led to the [[Syrian Democratic Forces]] eventually being integrated into the [[Syrian Army]], ending the [[Rojava Revolution]], while in Yemen, [[2025–2026 Southern Yemen campaign#Yemeni government counter-offensive|a counteroffensive]] by the Yemeni government led to the [[Fall of Aden (2026)|collapse]] of the [[Southern Transitional Council]]. In February, [[Pakistan]] launched airstrikes against [[Afghanistan]], leading to [[2026 Afghanistan–Pakistan war|an open war]] between both nations. In [[Iran]], in response to [[2025–2026 Iranian protests|authorities cracking down on protests]], the [[United States]] and [[Israel]] began [[2026 Iran war|a war]] in late February with the [[Assassination of Ali Khamenei|assassination of Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei]], which resulted in [[Economic impact of the 2026 Iran war|widespread economic grief]], [[2026 Iran war fuel crisis|fuel shortages]] and [[2026 Iranian strikes on Arab countries|retaliatory strikes]] on Gulf nations, as well as the [[2026 Strait of Hormuz crisis|closure of the Strait of Hormuz]] and [[2026 United States naval blockade of Iran|a naval blockade]]. There were also escalations in [[Yemen]] and [[Ethiopia]].

The continued release of the [[Epstein files]] that began the previous year has influenced scrutiny, media coverage, and in some cases arrests of high-profile individuals in politics, business, and academia in the Western world. Businessman [[Elon Musk]] became the first [[Trillionaire|person to have a verified net worth exceeding $1 trillion]] upon the [[Initial public offering of SpaceX|initial public offering of his company SpaceX]] in June.

In politics, a [[conservative wave]] spread through [[Latin America]], with [[right-wing politics|right-wing]] victories in [[2026 Colombian presidential election|Colombia]], [[2026 Costa Rican general election|Costa Rica]] and [[2026 Peruvian general election|Peru]]. Far-right parties in Europe continued to gain ground in the [[2026 United Kingdom local elections|United Kingdom]] and across the [[European Union]], though the long-ruling right-wing government in [[2026 Hungarian parliamentary election|Hungary lost power after ruling since 2010]], and [[2026 Portuguese presidential election|Portugal elected a centre-left social democrat over a right-wing populist in their presidential election]].

In science and technology, the dominant headline has been [[NASA]]'s [[Artemis II]] flyby of the Moon, the first crewed flight beyond [[low Earth orbit]] since [[Apollo 17]] in 1972, sending the first woman, Black man, and non-American beyond low Earth orbit. [[Artificial intelligence]] has continued to advance, with increasing focus on scaling models, infrastructure, and commercial applications. These developments have also brought growing scrutiny of the potential risks posed by increasingly capable AI systems, including concerns over [[AI alignment|alignment]], misuse, loss of human control, and their wider societal and economic effects. Notably, [[2026 cyberattacks by rogue OpenAI agents|autonomous cyberattacks]] conducted by [[OpenAI]]'s [[AI agent|AI agents]] during cybersecurity tests in mid-2026 led to major AI companies prioritizing the safety and alignment of their models.

In sports, the [[2026 Winter Olympics]] were the first [[Olympic Games]] to be officially co-hosted by two cities ([[Milan]] and [[Cortina d'Ampezzo]], [[Italy]]). The [[2026 FIFA World Cup]] became the first edition to be hosted by three nations ([[United States]], [[Mexico]] and [[Canada]]) and the first to include 48 teams, in addition to setting the record for the [[FIFA World Cup records and statistics#Attendance|highest attendance in World Cup history]]; a short time later, a crisis in FIFA erupted following a proposal of creation of a [[FIFA Forward Enterprise|commercial subsidiary]], largely rejected by confederations.

In seismology, earthquakes in the [[2026 Mindanao earthquake|Philippines]], [[2026 Venezuela earthquakes|Venezuela]], [[2026 Colombia earthquake|Colombia]] and [[2026 Flores earthquake|Indonesia]] caused widespread destruction; in nautics, several incidents, such as [[MV Hondius hantavirus outbreak|a hantavirus outbreak]] on the cruise ship [[MV Hondius|MV ''Hondius'']], the [[2026 Lake Kariba ferry accident|capsizing of the ferry ''Mbuya Nehanda'']] on [[Lake Kariba]], [[MV June Aster ship fire|a fire on the vessel MV ''June Aster'']] and the [[Sinking of the Virgo Transport 8|sinking of the ship ''Virgo Transport 8'']], resulted in large numbers of casualties; in mining, [[2026 Rubaya mines collapses|two collapses]] at the [[Rubaya mines]] in the [[Democratic Republic of the Congo]], [[2026 Liushenyu coal mine explosion|an explosion at a coal mine]] in [[China]] and [[2026 Kifula gold mine landslide|a landslide at a gold mine]] in [[Angola]] became some of the worst [[mining accident|disasters of their types]] in recent years of these countries.

In health, [[2026 Ebola epidemic|an epidemic]] of [[Bundibugyo virus|Ebola]] in the Democratic Republic of the Congo and [[Uganda]] has been declared a [[Public Health Emergency of International Concern]] by the [[World Health Organization]].

