* [[September 2]] – The [[2026 Estonian presidential election]]: [[Ülle Madise]] is elected [[president of Estonia]] by the [[Riigikogu]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-02-10 |title=Riigikogu presidential election votes set for early September |url=https://news.err.ee/1609937210/riigikogu-presidential-election-votes-set-for-early-september |access-date=2026-03-11 |website=ERR |language=en |archive-date=April 30, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260430024818/https://news.err.ee/1609937210/riigikogu-presidential-election-votes-set-for-early-september |url-status=live}}</ref>
* [[September 3]] – [[2026 Yemen offensives]]: The Houthis launch a large ground offensive against the internationally recognized Yemeni government forces, ending the ceasefire and resuming the [[Yemeni civil war (2014–present)|civil war]].
* [[September 4]] – The [[United Nations]] votes to adopt a resolution which calls for wider use of the [[Equal Earth projection]] over the [[Mercator projection]] to better reflect the true size of [[Tropics|equatorial regions]] such as [[Africa]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-09-05 |title=UN votes to adopt new world map to reflect Africa's true size |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ce30vp55dnlo |access-date=2026-09-20 |website=www.bbc.com |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=General Assembly Adopts Text to ‘Correct the Map’, among Others Proclaiming Decade of Culture for Sustainable Development, Child Marriage Elimination Day|url=https://press.un.org/en/2026/ga12779.doc.htm |access-date=2026-09-20 |website=press.un.org}}</ref> 
* [[September 4]]{{ndash}}[[September 13|13]] – The [[2026 FIBA Women's Basketball World Cup]] is held in [[Berlin]], Germany, and is won by the [[United States women's national basketball team|United States]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-09-04 | title=USA vs. China Box Score, Reaction from 2026 FIBA Women's World Cup Group Ahead of KO Bracket |url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/25496042-usa-vs-china-box-score-reaction-2026-fiba-womens-world-cup-group-ahead-ko-bracket |publisher=[[Bleacher Report]] |access-date=2026-09-04}}</ref>
* [[September 5]]–[[September 27|27]] – The [[2026 FIFA U-20 Women's World Cup]] is held in [[Łódź]], [[Katowice]], [[Sosnowiec]] and [[Bielsko-Biała]], Poland, and is won by [[Spain women's national under-20 football team|Spain]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/about-fifa/organisation/fifa-council/media-releases/fifa-council-appoints-chile-and-poland-as-hosts-of-fifa-youth-competitions|title=FIFA Council appoints Chile and Poland as hosts of FIFA youth competitions|publisher=[[FIFA]]|access-date=17 December 2023|date=17 December 2023|archive-date=March 3, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240303002308/https://www.fifa.com/about-fifa/organisation/fifa-council/media-releases/fifa-council-appoints-chile-and-poland-as-hosts-of-fifa-youth-competitions|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Polska gospodarzem Mistrzostw Świata Kobiet U-20 w 2026 roku! |url=https://pzpn.pl/federacja/aktualnosci/2023-12-17/polska-gospodarzem-mistrzostw-swiata-kobiet-u-20-w-2026-roku |access-date=18 December 2023 |publisher=[[Polish Football Association]] (PZPN) |date=17 December 2023 |language=pl |archive-date=January 23, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250123042608/https://pzpn.pl/federacja/aktualnosci/2023-12-17/polska-gospodarzem-mistrzostw-swiata-kobiet-u-20-w-2026-roku |url-status=live }}</ref>
* [[September 8]]
** Twelve countries, including Canada, France and the United Kingdom, announce plans to introduce or support trade restrictions on goods from [[Israeli settlement]]s, citing threats to a [[two-state solution]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/09/08/12-countries-including-france-uk-say-to-sanction-trade-from-israeli-settlements_6757291_4.html |title=12 countries including France, UK say to sanction trade from Israeli settlements |work=Le Monde |date=8 September 2026 |access-date=8 September 2026}}</ref>
** The [[Eighty-First session of the United Nations General Assembly]] opens in [[New York City|New York]].<ref>{{cite web |title=81st session of the UN General Assembly (UNGA 81) |url=https://unhabitat.org/events/81st-session-of-the-un-general-assembly-unga-81 |website=UN-Habitat |access-date=2 June 2026}}</ref>
* [[September 9]] – A [[MV June Aster ship fire|fire on the ferry MV ''June Aster'']] kills at least 76 people and 13 others go missing off [[Coron, Palawan]], [[Philippines]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Missing in Coron ferry fire down to 84; 2 passengers missed trip |url=https://www.philstar.com/nation/2026/09/11/2555574/missing-coron-ferry-fire-down-84-2-passengers-missed-trip |work=[[The Philippine Star]] |date=September 11, 2026|last=Ramirez|first=Renalyn|access-date=September 11, 2026}}</ref> 
* [[September 10]] – [[2026 Yemen offensives]]: The [[Houthis]] make significant advancements along [[Yemen]]'s [[Red Sea]] coast, [[Fall of Mokha|taking control]] of the city of [[Mokha]] and the strategic [[Strait of Bab al-Mandab|Bab al-Mandeb strait]], leading to a [[Bab al-Mandab Strait crisis|shipping crisis]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-09-11 |title=Houthis claim major advance in Yemen and tighten grip on Red Sea shipping lane |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c23x72yx2rvo |access-date=2026-09-15 |website=www.bbc.com |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Staff |first=Al Jazeera |title=Houthis control key shipping route after gains along Yemen's Red Sea coast |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/11/houthis-take-control-of-yemens-red-sea-coast-key-shipping-route |access-date=2026-09-15 |website=Al Jazeera |language=en}}</ref>
* [[September 10]]–[[September 11|11]] – [[2026 Iran war]]: Saudi Arabia temporarily shuts down the [[East–West Crude Oil Pipeline|East-West Pipeline]] following damage sustained from [[2026 East–West Crude Oil Pipeline attack|drone strikes]] launched from Iraqi territory.<ref>{{Cite web |title=MSN |url=https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/world/why-saudi-arabia-s-east-west-pipeline-matters-for-global-oil/ar-AA2ccag2 |access-date=2026-09-15 |website=www.msn.com}}</ref> 
* [[September 13]]
** [[2026 Swedish general election]]: The left-wing bloc led by former Prime Minister and [[Swedish Social Democratic Party|Social Democratic]] leader [[Magdalena Andersson]] narrowly wins a majority in the [[Riksdag]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-11-21 |title=Sweden braces for election hacks ahead of 2026 vote |url=https://www.politico.eu/article/sweden-braces-for-election-hacks-ahead-of-2026-vote/ |access-date=2026-01-18 |website=POLITICO |language=en-GB |archive-date=March 2, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260302012350/https://www.politico.eu/article/sweden-braces-for-election-hacks-ahead-of-2026-vote/ |url-status=live}}</ref>
** [[Sinking of the Virgo Transport 8|Indonesian ferry KM ''Virgo Transport 8'' sinks]] in the [[Java Sea]], killing 64 and leaving 71 missing.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Teresia |first1=Ananda |last2=Jalil |first2=Awaluddin |date=2026-09-18 |title=Indonesia recovers three bodies as effort begins to right capsized ship |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/indonesia-begins-operation-right-capsized-ship-java-sea-129-remain-missing-2026-09-18/ |access-date=2026-09-19 |work=Reuters |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Virgo Transport 8 - Passenger ship, IMO 8625179, MMSI 525110543, Callsign JVUY8, Flag Indonesia - vesseltracker.com |url=https://www.vesseltracker.com/en/Ships/Virgo-Transport-8-8625179.html |access-date=2026-09-26 |website=www.vesseltracker.com}}</ref>
* [[September 14]] – The United States publicly acknowledges for the first time that it has deployed "space control weapons" in [[Geocentric orbit|Earth orbit]], with [[United States Secretary of the Air Force|Air Force Secretary]] [[Troy Meink]] confirming that the [[United States Space Force]] possesses orbital weapons capable of offensive and defensive operations.<ref>{{cite news |last=Copp |first=Tara |title=U.S. has deployed first space-based weapon, Air Force secretary says |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/national-security/2026/09/14/us-has-deployed-first-space-based-weapon-air-force-secretary-says/ |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=14 September 2026 |access-date=15 September 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Clark |first=Stephen |title=For the first time, the US military confirms it has deployed weapons in orbit |url=https://arstechnica.com/space/2026/09/for-the-first-time-the-us-military-confirms-it-has-deployed-weapons-in-orbit/ |work=Ars Technica |date=14 September 2026 |access-date=15 September 2026}}</ref>

* [[September 16]] – Former [[president of Kosovo|president]] and [[prime minister of Kosovo]] [[Hashim Thaçi]] is sentenced to 25 years in prison for [[War crimes in the Kosovo War#KLA war crimes|war crimes during his command]] of the [[Kosovo Liberation Army]], including the murder of 96 people and the torture of 303 people in the [[Kosovo War]].<ref name="KSC_Thaci_found_guilty">{{cite Q|Q141541247|url-status=live}}</ref>
* [[September 17]] – ''[[Tilcayo|Leopardus tilcayo]]'', a wild [[Felidae|cat]] native to [[Bolivia]], is reported as the first new feline species formally identified since 1923.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Geggel |first1=Laura |last2=Mondragón |first2=Laura |date=2026-09-17 |title=Scientists name a new cat species for the first time in over a century |url=https://www.livescience.com/animals/cats/scientists-name-a-new-cat-species-for-the-first-time-in-over-a-century |access-date=2026-09-19 |website=Live Science |language=en}}</ref>
* [[September 18]] 
** Following months of threats by U.S. President [[Donald Trump]] to [[Greenland crisis|acquire or annex Greenland]], an agreement is announced by [[Denmark]], [[Greenland]] and the United States to strengthen security in the [[Arctic]] and North [[Atlantic Ocean|Atlantic]], expanding the permanent U.S. military presence in Greenland while recognizing Danish sovereignty and Greenlandic self-determination.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-09-18 |title=US and Denmark reach deal over Greenland's security after Trump threats |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c63d7lexyym1o |access-date=2026-09-21 |website=BBC News |language=en-GB}}</ref> The agreement is formally signed on [[September 22]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/watch-trump-signs-agreement-with-leaders-of-denmark-and-greenland |access-date=2026-09-22 |title = Trump Signs Agreement with Leaders of Denmark and Greenland |website=www.pbs.org}}</ref>
** [[2026 French lycée protests]]: A series of student led [[Protest|protests]] begin in the outskirts of [[Paris]] with students demanding better funding and resources for schools. In early [[October]] these protests had spread across [[France]] and into neighboring [[Belgium]], with hundreds of thousands taking part and thousands arrested.<ref>{{Cite web |title=‘Gen Z has risen up’: Tens of thousands mass for tense France school protests |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/world/gen-z-has-risen-up-tens-of-thousands-mass-for-tense-france-school-protests/M45MNNTHR5DMJBXMFWITMKFROM/ |access-date=2026-10-11 |website=NZ Herald |language=en-NZ}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Balintec |first=Vanessa |last2=Lima |first2=Lioman |last3=Balintec |first3=Vanessa |date=2026-10-08 |title=Police clash with protesters in Paris as French high school students rally over conditions - as it happened |url=https://www.reuters.com/live/france-protests-live-high-school-students-rally-over-conditions-tensions-flare-2026-10-08/ |access-date=2026-10-13 |work=Reuters |language=en}}</ref>
* [[September 18]]–[[September 20|20]] – [[2026 Russian legislative election]]: The ruling [[United Russia]] led by [[Dmitry Medvedev]] and supported by president [[Vladimir Putin]] maintains parliamentary majority in an election which saw no significant competition.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Результаты — сводка |url=https://neshodilina.netlify.app/results?p=2 |access-date=2026-09-22 |website=neshodilina.netlify.app}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-09-21 |title=Russia's elections had few surprises - but how the Kremlin uses the results will be crucial |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cx4gqv239043o |access-date=2026-09-22 |website=www.bbc.com |language=en-GB}}</ref> Western media outlets criticise the election as being rigged or interfered with.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Editorial |date=2026-09-13 |title=The Guardian view on Russian parliamentary elections: glimpses of weakness behind the facade |url=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/sep/13/the-guardian-view-on-russian-parliamentary-elections-glimpses-of-weakness-behind-the-facade |access-date=2026-09-26 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-09-21 |title=Arrests and opposition parties banned: How Putin controlled Russia's 'sham' elections |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/europe/russian-elections-putin-ukraine-duma-b3053446.html |access-date=2026-09-26 |website=The Independent |language=en}}</ref>
* [[September 19]]{{ndash}}[[October 4]] – The [[2026 Asian Games]] are held around [[Aichi Prefecture]] in Japan.<ref>{{cite news|title=2026 Asian Games to be held in autumn to avoid summer heat in Japan|url=http://www.xinhuanet.com/english/2018-08/19/c_137402371.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181023080247/http://www.xinhuanet.com/english/2018-08/19/c_137402371.htm|url-status=dead|archive-date=October 23, 2018|access-date=23 October 2018|work=Xinhuanet|publisher=xinhuanet.com|date=19 August 2018}}</ref>
* [[September 23]]
** [[2026 Moroccan general election]]: The [[Authenticity and Modernity Party]] led by [[Fatima Ezzahra El Mansouri]] wins the most seats in the [[House of Representatives (Morocco)|House of Representatives]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Moroccan General Election - House of Representatives, 2026 |url=https://poliglobe.com/election/1311 |website=Poliglobe}}</ref> Fatima Ezzahra El Mansouri becomes [[Morocco]]'s first female prime minister.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Staff |first=Al Jazeera |title=‘Truly historic’: Morocco’s king appoints first woman prime minister |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/29/truly-historic-moroccos-king-appoints-first-woman-prime-minister |access-date=2026-09-29 |website=Al Jazeera |language=en}}</ref>
** [[2026 Ethiopian offensive]]: The [[Tigray People's Liberation Front|Tigray People’s Liberation Front]] and groups aligned with them launch a military offensive in northern [[Ethiopia]] against the [[Federal government of Ethiopia|Ethiopian Government]], resuming the [[Ethiopian civil conflict (2018–present)|civil conflict]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Staff |first=Al Jazeera |title=Tigray forces launch offensive against Ethiopian government |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/23/ethiopian-airlines-suspends-flights-to-three-northern-tigray-airports |access-date=2026-09-24 |website=Al Jazeera |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-09-25 |title=Ethiopia fighting: Are Tigray's TPLF forces and the army heading back to war? |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c6d94wywx0ypo |access-date=2026-09-26 |website=www.bbc.com |language=en-GB}}</ref> 
* [[September 24]]–[[September 30|30]] – [[2026 Bangkok floods]]: At least 40 are killed during flooding in [[Bangkok]], [[Thailand]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Thai floods disrupt factories, capital still at risk, PM says |url=https://www.channelnewsasia.com/asia/thai-floods-capital-still-risk-6421491 |access-date=2026-10-01 |website=CNA |language=en}}</ref>
* [[September 25]] – 41 people die when a boat sinks in [[Lake Tanganyika]] in the [[Democratic Republic of the Congo]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Staff |first=Al Jazeera |title=At least 41 die as boat capsizes in DRC’s Lake Tanganyika |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/25/at-least-41-die-as-boat-capsizes-in-drcs-lake-tanganyika |access-date=2026-09-28 |website=Al Jazeera |language=en}}</ref>
* [[September 25]]–[[September 27|27]] – [[Flash flood|Flash flooding]] and [[Landslide|landslides]] kill at least 56 people in [[India]] and at least 14 people in [[Nepal]]. For Nepal this occurs one month on from [[2026 Nepal–Tibet floods|devastating floods]].<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Dash |first1=Jatindra |last2=Sharma |first2=Gopal |date=2026-09-27 |title=Floods, landslides kill 56 in India and 14 in Nepal |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/environment/floods-landslides-kill-15-india-four-nepal-rivers-rise-2026-09-27/ |access-date=2026-09-28 |work=Reuters |language=en}}</ref>
* [[September 27]] – [[2026 São Toméan parliamentary election]]: The [[Movement for the Liberation of São Tomé and Príncipe – Social Democratic Party|MLSTP–PSD]] led by Américo Barros wins the most seats in the [[National Assembly (São Tomé and Príncipe)|National Assembly]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sao Tome Legislative Election, 2026 |url=https://www.poliglobe.com/election/1390 |website=Poliglobe}}</ref>

=== October ===
[[File:CCTV vision of Houthi attacks on Saudi airport.webm|thumb| CCTV footage of the [[October 2026 attacks on Saudi Arabia airports|missile strike]] on [[King Khalid International Airport]] on October 10]]
* [[October 2]] – [[G7]] leaders hold an emergency meeting to discuss global [[diesel fuel|diesel]] supplies and agree to release 100 million barrels of oil, as prices surge to record highs amid disruption from the [[2026 Iran war]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/live/cqd0801d2y3vt |title=G7 leaders hold urgent meeting over diesel supply as UK prices hit record £2 a litre |date=2 October 2026 |website=BBC News |publisher=BBC |accessdate=2 October 2026}}</ref>
* [[October 3]] – [[2026 Latvian parliamentary election]]: Incumbent Prime Minister [[Andris Kulbergs]]'s [[United List]] wins a plurality of seats in the [[Saeima|Saeima.]]<ref>{{Cite web |title=Latvian Saeima elections 2026: Myths and Misconceptions |url=https://eng.lsm.lv/article/politics/election/04.09.2026-latvian-saeima-elections-2026-myths-and-misconceptions.a661764/ |access-date=2026-09-04 |website=eng.lsm.lv |language=en}}</ref>
* [[October 4]] – The [[2026 Bosnian general election]] is held.<ref name="Bosnia">{{Cite web|url=https://www.izbori.ba/en/elections/2026|title=Stranica / dokument ne postoje|website=www.izbori.ba|access-date=April 29, 2026|archive-date=April 30, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260430003735/https://www.izbori.ba/en/elections/2026|url-status=live}}</ref>
* [[October 5]] – [[2026 Yemen offensives|Houthi-Saudi conflict]][[2026 Yemen offensives|:]] [[Turkey]], [[Pakistan]] and [[Saudi Arabia]] agree to activate the [[Mecca Joint Defence Agreement|Mecca defense pact]] in response to [[Houthis|Houthi]] attacks on Saudi Arabia.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Mecca alliance agrees to activate collective deterrence measures |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/5/mecca-alliance-agrees-to-activate-collective-deterrence-measures |access-date=2026-10-05 |website=Al Jazeera |language=en}}</ref>
* [[October 6]] – [[2026 Kosovan presidential election]]: The [[Assembly of Kosovo]] elects [[Justina Shiroka Pula]] as the new [[President of Kosovo]], ending the [[2025–2026 Kosovo political deadlock|2025–2026 political deadlock]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Kosovo Elects President Dodging New Political Deadlock |work=Prishtina Insight |url=https://prishtinainsight.com/kosovo-elects-president-dodging-new-political-deadlock/ |date=7 October 2026}}</ref>
* [[October 6]]–[[October 12|12]] – [[Houthi–Saudi conflict|Houthi-Saudi conflict]]: At least 20 people are killed and more than 350 others are injured in a [[October 2026 attacks on Saudi Arabia airports|series of attacks on airports]] perpetrated by the [[Houthis]] in [[Riyadh]], [[Abha]] and [[Dammam]], Saudi Arabia.<ref name=":2">{{Cite news |last=Nereim|first=Vivian|last2=Ward|first2=Euan|date=2026-10-10|title=Saudi Arabia’s Riyadh Airport Attacked Again, After Days of Houthi Strikes|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/10/world/middleeast/riyadh-airport-attack-energy-congress.html|access-date=2026-10-10|work=The New York Times|language=en-US|issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-10-11 |title=Saudi Arabia's Riyadh airport hit by second Houthi strike in two days |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cr93znyk44e1o |access-date=2026-10-11 |website=www.bbc.com |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url= https://abcnews.com/International/live-updates/iran-live-updates-trump-us-attack-iran-ahead?id=137063637&entryId=137188731&utm_source=facebook&utm_medium=social&utm_campaign=dhfacebook&utm_content=null|title= At least 20 killed in week of attacks on Saudi airports, officials say |work=ABC News|date=2026-10-12|access-date=2026-10-12}}</ref>
* [[October 8]]–[[October 9|9]] – [[2026 Djibouti migrant boat sinking]]: At least 139 people die and 84 remain missing when a migrant boat travelling to [[Yemen]] sinks off of [[Djibouti]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mureithi |first=Carlos |date=2026-10-11 |title=At least 139 dead including children after migrant boat capsizes off Djibouti |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/11/deaths-migrant-boat-capsizes-djibouti |access-date=2026-10-11 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>
* [[October 11]]–[[October 12|12]] – [[2026 French supermarket refrigerator hack]]: A major [[cyberattack]] targets the [[Refrigeration|refrigeration units]] of hundreds of supermarkets across [[France]], raising the temperature to {{convert|50|°C|°F|1}}. The hack results in fires and tens of millions of [[Euro|euros]] worth of losses from [[food spoilage]]. Other countries in [[Europe]] are also impacted to a lesser extent. It is the first cyberattack to cause major disruption to a nation's food [[supply chain]].<ref>{{Cite news|date=2026-10-12 |title=Hackers force French supermarket fridges to heat up to 50C in ‘unprecedented’ cyber-attack |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/12/cyber-attack-defrosts-refrigerators-france-supermarket-chains-intermarche-cooperative-u |access-date=2026-10-15 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Marchandon |first=Leo |last2=Stargardter |first2=Gabriel |last3=Marchandon |first3=Leo |date=2026-10-12 |title=Hackers heat French supermarket fridges, sparking fires and spoiling food |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/retail-consumer/french-supermarket-chain-intermarche-hit-by-cyberattack-150-stories-targeted-2026-10-12/ |access-date=2026-10-15 |work=Reuters |language=en}}</ref>
* [[October 12]]–[[October 18|18]] – [[G20]] finance ministers meet with the [[International Monetary Fund]] and the [[World Bank Group|World Bank]] in [[Thailand]] to discuss the [[Economic impact of the 2026 Iran war|economic fallout of the Iran War]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Power |first=John |title=G20 finance ministers, central bankers gather amid fallout from Iran war |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/10/15/g20-finance-ministers-central-bankers-gather-amid-fallout-from-iran-war |access-date=2026-10-15 |website=Al Jazeera |language=en}}</ref>
* [[October 13]] – An early [[Bronze Age|bronze age]] [[shipwreck]] off the coast of [[Turkey]] which dates between 2400–2700 BC is reported as the [[Kekova shipwreck|world's oldest shipwreck]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-10-14 |title=World's oldest shipwreck found off Kekova Island in Turkey |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c6gr80rk1yyko |access-date=2026-10-15 |website=www.bbc.com |language=en-GB}}</ref>
*[[October 16]] – At the [[General Conference on Weights and Measures]], the [[International Bureau of Weights and Measures]] votes to abolish future [[leap second]]s and make [[Coordinated Universal Time]] (UTC) continuous, starting 20 May 2027. To account for future differences between UTC and [[International Atomic Time]], a leap hour will be observed as needed.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sample |first1=Ian |title=How an accelerating Earth led experts to adopt the leap hour |url=https://www.theguardian.com/science/2026/oct/15/times-up-for-the-leap-second-as-scientists-swap-it-for-the-leap-hour |access-date=15 October 2026 |work=The Guardian |date=15 October 2026}}</ref>

